00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.460
Hello and welcome back to
another video with ItsDrDan.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.590
And today we're going to be learning
about how to classify aldoses and

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.019
ketoses, what you will, which is
something that you're going to

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.779
find in introductory biochemistry.

00:00:19.309 --> 00:00:25.290
So with these, these are carbohydrates
that have aldehyde and ketone groups.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.430
So whenever we are seeing an aldose,
so for the first example, all the way

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:34.350
up top, You can see that we have the
aldehyde, which is all the way on carbon

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.960
number one for the first structure.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.680
So let's just write this one down here.

00:00:39.179 --> 00:00:45.645
And then for the, uh, ketone, we can
see that on carbon Number two, we always

00:00:45.655 --> 00:00:48.435
have our ketone with biomolecules.

00:00:48.795 --> 00:00:52.175
So we're going to go through step
by step how we can actually classify

00:00:52.295 --> 00:00:56.775
these as aldoses, ketoses, what exactly
are they, and how we can count the

00:00:56.775 --> 00:01:00.305
number of carbons in these different
structures and how we can label

00:01:00.305 --> 00:01:02.854
them for your biochemistry course.

00:01:02.914 --> 00:01:04.014
Let's take a look at it.

00:01:09.324 --> 00:01:12.935
All right, so the first thing is when
we are classifying carbohydrates,

00:01:12.955 --> 00:01:15.875
these are known as simple sugars.

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:19.530
because these are
specifically monosaccharides.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.220
So whenever we have a monosaccharide,
that means there is one single

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.589
carbohydrate that we are showing.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.759
Now with most of these
different carbohydrates,

00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:31.539
they have the ending of ose.

00:01:31.849 --> 00:01:35.429
So whenever we see that ose ending,
that's telling you, hey, this is a

00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:39.579
sugar, this is a carbohydrate, um,
being that it has the carbon chain,

00:01:39.589 --> 00:01:41.729
it has that aldehyde or ketone.

00:01:42.199 --> 00:01:47.240
And it has those alternating OHs that
we will see in Fischer projections or

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:50.250
Hayworth projections to illustrate these.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.949
Let's take a look even farther into this.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.000
So when we are given a carbohydrate
or a simple monosaccharide, the

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.129
first step is that you have to
identify the functional group.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.950
which is what you're going to, what you
will receive training on how to do from

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.680
an, from your introductory organic class.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.810
So with this, what you're looking
for is two major functional groups.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.820
These are aldehydes and ketones.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:18.690
So when we have our first one, so with our
aldehyde, what's going to happen is we're

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:25.220
going to take that ALD portion and put
the ose ending on it to make it an aldose.

00:02:25.915 --> 00:02:32.034
With ketose or ketone, we take ketone
and then we transport that into ketose.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.950
Now, the reason why I highlighted these
specific portions of the biochem is that

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:42.040
when you actually classify these, you're
going to use the aldo or keto to help

00:02:42.079 --> 00:02:47.179
explain exactly what kind of carbohydrate
or simple sugar you are looking at.

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:51.070
And always remember the aldehyde or the
aldose is always going to be on carbon

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:56.160
number one and the ketose will always be
on carbon number two for simple sugars.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:57.760
Let's go to step number two.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.060
Step two, now that we identified
what functional group we have.

00:03:02.375 --> 00:03:04.655
We're now going to count
the number of carbons.

00:03:05.085 --> 00:03:09.905
So there are generally most of these
simple carbohydrates go up to typically

00:03:09.905 --> 00:03:12.515
seven carbons most of the time.

00:03:12.865 --> 00:03:16.935
So if we have, let's say three
carbons, this is going to be a

00:03:17.004 --> 00:03:21.875
trios, so, or trios, so think of
the triangle, a triangle, so trios.

00:03:22.554 --> 00:03:28.915
Now, when we have four, this is a
tetros, five is pentos, six is hexos.

00:03:29.545 --> 00:03:34.234
So for the next portion, we are
going to combine step one and two,

00:03:34.795 --> 00:03:38.844
which being that we have the aldehyde
and ketone from the first step.

00:03:38.844 --> 00:03:43.094
So we're going to take aldo or keto,
depending on the functional group,

00:03:43.605 --> 00:03:47.185
and we're going to combine that with
the number of carbons that we have.

00:03:47.615 --> 00:03:51.635
So if I have, let's say, the example here,
what you're going to do is we're going to

00:03:51.644 --> 00:03:56.535
first do is identify the functional group
and what we can see up here is that, oh,

00:03:56.575 --> 00:03:59.484
well, we have an aldo or an aldehyde.

00:03:59.625 --> 00:04:00.095
Okay.

00:04:00.405 --> 00:04:02.494
So that would be the first portion here.

00:04:02.494 --> 00:04:05.294
So let's write aldehyde is what we have.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.899
Uh, as our functional group.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.170
So it's gonna be Aldo.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.930
Now how many carbons does it have?

00:04:12.989 --> 00:04:15.420
So what we will do is we
are going to number, right?

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.610
We have 1, 2, 3.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.810
We start numbering all the way at
the top of our Fisher projection.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.240
We number down.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.060
So start all the way as close to
the most oxidized carbon as you

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.063
can to the aldehyde or the ketone.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.840
So now that we have three carbons,
this is going to be an Aldo trios.

00:04:34.245 --> 00:04:38.005
as our classification for this structure.

00:04:38.055 --> 00:04:42.005
Okay, so that is all you have to do
is when you are classifying them.

00:04:42.545 --> 00:04:45.275
Let's go through a couple
different examples and practice

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:46.455
these different skills.

00:04:46.765 --> 00:04:47.795
Let's take a look.

00:04:48.094 --> 00:04:50.724
So with the following questions,
we're going to classify the

00:04:50.725 --> 00:04:52.174
following carbohydrates.

00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:54.065
So we're going to go
through our steps, right?

00:04:54.065 --> 00:04:55.325
Step one plus two.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.310
putting our two things together.

00:04:57.849 --> 00:05:02.430
So the very first step is that we
need to count and classify how many,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.689
classify what our functional group is.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:09.280
So on the structure on the left
we can see that oh we have an

00:05:09.349 --> 00:05:12.609
aldehyde, right, because we are
looking right here on carbon number

00:05:12.609 --> 00:05:14.999
one we see that C double bond O.

00:05:15.634 --> 00:05:17.564
So that means that this will be an aldo.

00:05:18.044 --> 00:05:20.104
Now, how many carbons do we have?

00:05:20.544 --> 00:05:21.794
Well, we're going to go down our chain.

00:05:21.794 --> 00:05:29.054
We see we have one, two, three, four,
five, six carbons on our structure.

00:05:29.724 --> 00:05:31.634
So now what is that going to be?

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:36.724
Well, in this case, it's going to be
an aldo hexose for our title, right?

00:05:36.724 --> 00:05:39.315
So we can see we have hex for six.

00:05:39.815 --> 00:05:41.775
So it's going to be aldo hexose.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:47.760
So and that will be how we would
classify the structure on the left.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.050
Now what about the structure above?

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.400
Well same idea, we gotta first
identify what the functional group is.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:01.069
And when we go through numbering here,
right, one, two, three, four, five, we can

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:06.520
see that on carbon number two we have our
carbonyl, right, so our C double bond O.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.169
So what that means is when we
have that there we have a keto.

00:06:12.745 --> 00:06:18.135
And being that there's five carbons,
that's going to be ketopentose, is

00:06:18.135 --> 00:06:20.565
going to be the name for that structure.

00:06:21.015 --> 00:06:24.155
Okay, or sorry, not name, it's going to
be the classification for that structure.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.610
Let's take a look at another exam,
another two examples and keep

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:29.930
practicing it for our last two.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:33.489
So if this is something that you want
to practice, pause the video, see if

00:06:33.489 --> 00:06:36.590
you can do it on yourself for yourself
before I go through the example.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.900
So we're going to start by class.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:40.979
So number our carbon.

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:46.020
So we got one, two, three, four,
and we see that on our carbon

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:48.390
number two, we have a carbonyl.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:53.020
So that's C double bondo that tells
you, Hey, we have a keto, right?

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:53.830
Or ketone.

00:06:54.499 --> 00:06:57.260
So it's going to be keto and
being that we have four of them.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.820
This is going to be Ketotetros
for our classification.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.849
We're going to do the same
thing on the next one, right?

00:07:04.849 --> 00:07:07.770
One, two, three, four, five.

00:07:08.289 --> 00:07:12.030
And what we see is, oh, there's a
C double bond O all the way up top.

00:07:12.509 --> 00:07:17.479
So this is going to be aldo and
tos, being that there are five.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.930
And that would be how
you classify this one.

